[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the “invisible bridge” holding the two halves together and confirmed it is an OSPF virtual-link between r4 and r8. That virtual link is presently healthy. Evidence: on r4, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows neighbor 18.1.4.1 on interface VLINK0 in Full state, and “show ip ospf” reports “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 1.” On r8, “show ip ospf neighbor” shows neighbor 14.1.4.1 on VLINK0 Full, with “show ip ospf” likewise reporting one full virtual adjacency via area 1. Intra-area adjacencies (r4–r5–r6–r8) are Full, and the redistribution bridge to the EIGRP island is up: r11 has an OSPF neighbor to r1 (Full) and EIGRP neighbors to r12 and r13. End-to-end reachability is good: pc0 (20.1.1.10) ↔ pc12 (120.1.1.10) ping succeeds with 0% loss and ~2–3 ms RTT. r12’s routing shows 120.0.0.0/8 connected on eth1 and EIGRP/OSPF exchange functioning; r1’s OSPF table contains the remote networks (classful /8s).

Mechanism: if this virtual link were down (e.g., immediately after a reboot before FRR/OSPF formed), the backbone would be discontiguous and inter-area routes would withdraw, making the far side “fall off the map.” That condition is not present now.

Root cause: No active fault—OSPF virtual link r4↔r8 (VLINK0) is established and routing is converged.

Corrective fix: None required. To harden against recurrence, ensure frr is enabled at boot on r4/r8 and keep stable OSPF router-ids; consider replacing the virtual-link with a direct backbone adjacency if feasible.
=== END ===